1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,233
Calling a function takes six basic steps.

2
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:13,633
First, put the arguments where the function can find them, then jump there…

3
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:17,733
…the function sets up its own local storage and runs its body…

4
00:00:17,733 --> 00:00:23,500
…puts the return value in place, and finally jumps back to where it was called.

5
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:31,266
Here's the simplest example: a = sum(a, b), with a in s0 and b in s1.

6
00:00:31,266 --> 00:00:38,800
Step one: put the arguments in a0 and a1. The return value will come back in a0, too.

7
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:48,066
jal means jump and link: it saves the address of the next instruction, PC + 4, in ra, then jumps to sum.

8
00:00:48,066 --> 00:00:51,366
The function computes its result into a0…

9
00:00:51,366 --> 00:00:59,133
…then jr ra jumps back to the address saved in ra, 0x100C, and execution carries on.

10
00:00:59,133 --> 00:01:06,033
Why not just j back? Because sum may be called from many places; only ra knows where to return this time.

11
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:14,566
jr ra is shorthand for jalr x0, 0(ra), and is often written as ret.

12
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:20,466
Here's the catch: there's only one set of registers, shared by the caller and the callee.

13
00:01:20,466 --> 00:01:27,366
But f needs registers too. If it overwrites a value the caller still needs, that data is silently clobbered.

14
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:32,266
So everyone agrees on a set of rules, called the calling convention.

15
00:01:32,266 --> 00:01:41,133
The t and a registers and ra are caller-saved: the callee may change them freely. If the caller needs them afterward, it must save them itself.

16
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:49,866
The s registers and sp are callee-saved: a function that uses one must save the old value first and restore it before returning.

17
00:01:49,866 --> 00:01:57,900
In other words: across a function call, the s registers are guaranteed to keep their values; the t and a registers are not.

18
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:03,700
So where do these old values go? Into a region of memory called the stack.

19
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:12,266
A program's memory is split into a few regions: code, static data, the heap, and, up at the high addresses, the stack.

20
00:02:12,266 --> 00:02:23,033
The stack grows down, from high addresses toward low ones. sp, the stack pointer, points to the top of the stack: the lowest address in use.

21
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:29,366
Let's zoom in. Suppose a function needs to save two registers, ra and s0.

22
00:02:29,366 --> 00:02:33,866
To push, first subtract 8 from sp to make room for two words…

23
00:02:33,866 --> 00:02:41,300
…then store the registers with sw: ra at sp + 4, s0 at sp + 0.

24
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:45,366
Now the function is free to change ra and s0.

25
00:02:45,366 --> 00:02:53,966
Before returning, pop in reverse order: lw brings back the old values, then sp is moved back up.

26
00:02:53,966 --> 00:03:03,500
sp is back where it started, and the registers hold their old values. The saved data isn't erased; it just no longer belongs to anyone.

27
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:08,533
Finally, a complete example: sumSquare calls another function, mult.

28
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:17,000
On entry, x = 3 is in a0, y = 5 is in a1, and ra holds the caller's return address.

29
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:26,666
sumSquare itself calls mult with jal, and jal overwrites ra. Without saving ra first, it could never get back to its caller.

30
00:03:26,666 --> 00:03:35,266
y must go on the stack too: a1 is about to carry x, and since the a registers are caller-saved, mult may change a1 as well.

31
00:03:35,266 --> 00:03:40,733
First comes the prologue: make room for two words, then save ra and y.

32
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:52,100
Set up the arguments for mult(3, 3), then jal: ra now holds the address of the instruction after the jal, here 0x2014.

33
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:58,133
After mult returns, there's no telling what's in a1. Luckily, y is safe on the stack.

34
00:03:58,133 --> 00:04:08,200
That gives 9 + 5 = 14. Then the epilogue: restore ra, add 8 back to sp, and jr ra returns to the caller.

35
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:15,466
The prologue saves the state; the epilogue restores it. Almost every function that calls another has this shape.

